Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to recreate a
Python script that synchronizes the local master branch of a Git repository with
an upstream branch on origin, covering purpose, inputs and outputs,
dependencies, logic, error handling, edge cases, and usage.
Do not include code or snippets.
Create a single-file Python 3 command-line script (shebang pointing to the
Termux Python interpreter at /data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python)
that synchronizes the current local Git branch with an upstream branch fetched
from the "origin" remote, using the GitPython library (imported as the `git`
package).
Start the script with the `from __future__ import annotations` import, then
import `sys` and `Repo` from `git`.
Define one main function, `sync_branch_with_upstream`, that accepts an optional
repository path string defaulting to the current directory and returns a boolean
indicating success.
Wrap the body in a try block for error handling.
Behavior to implement, in order: 1.
Open the repository at the given path with `Repo`. 2.
Check the active branch name.
If it is not "master", print a warning that includes the current branch name,
then prompt the user via `input` with the question "Continue anyway? (y/n): ".
If the response, lowercased, is not "y", return False immediately. 3.
Print "Fetching from origin..." and call fetch on the `origin` remote. 4.
Determine the upstream reference: prefer "origin/master" if it exists in the
repository's refs; otherwise use "origin/main" if it exists; otherwise print an
error stating that neither origin/master nor origin/main exists and return
False. 5.
Print which upstream branch is being used. 6.
Resolve the upstream reference's commit object. 7.
Print "Rebasing with unrelated histories..." and record the current active
branch object and the current HEAD commit. 8.
Run a git rebase through the GitPython `git` interface with the `--onto` option
set to the upstream commit, using the recorded HEAD commit as the starting point
and the current branch as the branch to rebase, passing the
`allow_unrelated_histories=True` keyword so unrelated histories are permitted.
The inner rebase call must be guarded by its own try/except so that a rebase
failure can be handled separately from other errors (for example, by reporting
the failure and aborting or restoring the original state, and returning False).
Outer error handling: catch any other exception raised during the overall
process, print a descriptive error message including the exception text, and
return False.
Implement a standard entry point guard so the script runs when executed
directly.
Pass the first command-line argument (if provided, from `sys.argv`) as the
repository path, defaulting to the current directory.
Exit with status 0 if the function returns True, and exit with status 1
otherwise.
Notable details and edge cases to handle: - The script is interactive when not
on master; it blocks on user input. - Only the "origin" remote is used; its
absence will raise an error caught by the outer handler. - Both master and main
are supported as upstream, with master taking precedence. - The rebase uses
unrelated-history permission, so it can combine histories that share no common
ancestor. - Output is plain text printed to standard output; warnings and errors
are printed with "Warning:" and "Error:" prefixes. - Keep the code concise,
idiomatic, and consistent in style, using f-strings for formatted messages, and
add no features beyond those described. ---
